#
# arch/os — the platform layer's configuration, kbuild's own.
#
# The code under arch/os/<platform> acts on a process rather than on data: the
# system call without a C library to make it through, the call randomness is
# drawn with, where the system call instructions in a run of code are, and (on
# the platforms that have it) arming them in this process. It is kbuild's, not
# any one consumer's — the same system call on the same kernel for every tree
# that vendors kbuild — so its menu lives here, sourced in one line by a tree
# that builds the layer rather than copied into each tree's own Kconfig.
#
# PLATFORM is a fact about the build, not a choice: kbuild derives it from the
# target triple before the configuration is read (scripts/Makefile.target), so
# the layer that is offered is the one that could be built — arch/os/linux,
# arch/os/macos or arch/os/windows — and the symbols under it (OS_IO,
# OS_ENTROPY, OS_SCAN, OS_SC) are named for what they are rather than for the
# kernel that answers them, so a consumer writes `select OS_IO` and gets the
# platform's. A target with no layer here has no directory to source and the
# build fails on that, rather than quietly configuring nothing.
#
# This is sourced by the trees that build the platform layer — ub, and a
# standalone kbuild build for its own selftests — not by every tree that
# vendors kbuild: a tree that does not build arch/os (un, whose logger falls
# back to the C library) must not offer these symbols, because setting OS_IO
# without building arch/os/<platform>/io leaves the _sys_ calls its callers
# reference undefined at link time.
#
menu "Platform services"

source "arch/os/$PLATFORM/Kconfig"

endmenu
